-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 — theft.
--
--   s 378  defines "theft"                                (the elements)
--   s 379  punishes whoever commits theft                 (the charge)
--   s 380  punishes theft in a building, tent or vessel   (the charge)
--
-- A charge is laid under the PUNISHING section, so this module exports two
-- charges (ss 379 and 380) over two facts records, and one offence ladder over
-- the s 378 elements. The s 380 record NESTS the s 378 one: s 380 adds a place
-- to a theft, it does not restate one.
--
-- s 378 is the parent of the whole property-offence family in this row. It is
-- IMPORTed by robbery-390-392.l4, which nests a `Theft Facts` inside its own
-- record because s 390(1) says "In all robbery there is either theft or
-- extortion" — robbery adds an aggravation to a theft, it does not restate one.
--
-- An ISOMORPHIC encoding in INERT STYLE — see culpable-homicide-301.l4 for the
-- conventions and cheating-415-417-420.l4 for the charge machinery. Verbatim
-- statutory prose rides as inert strings; only the operative atoms are lifted
-- into named leaves; the inert strings are mortar, never shadow copies of the
-- leaf beside them.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The text
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- 378. Whoever, intending to take dishonestly any movable property out of the
--      possession of any person without that person's consent, moves that
--      property in order to such taking, is said to commit theft.
--      Explanation 1.—A thing so long as it is attached to the earth, not being
--      movable property, is not the subject of theft; but it becomes capable of
--      being the subject of theft as soon as it is severed from the earth.
--      Explanation 2.—A moving, effected by the same act which effects the
--      severance, may be a theft.
--      Explanation 3.—A person is said to cause a thing to move by removing an
--      obstacle which prevented it from moving, or by separating it from any
--      other thing, as well as by actually moving it.
--      Explanation 4.—A person, who by any means causes an animal to move, is
--      said to move that animal, and to move everything which in consequence of
--      the motion so caused is moved by that animal.
--      Explanation 5.—The consent mentioned in the definition may be express or
--      implied, and may be given either by the person in possession, or by any
--      person having for that purpose authority either express or implied.
--      Explanation 6.—A person may move property which is intangible or
--      incorporeal by transferring the property from one account to another
--      account or transferring the interests in the property from one person to
--      another person or by extinguishing the property.
--                                                                    [15/2019]
--
-- 379. Whoever commits theft shall be punished with imprisonment for a term
--      which may extend to 3 years, or with fine, or with both.
--
-- 380. Whoever commits theft in any building, tent or vessel, which building,
--      tent or vessel is used as a human dwelling, or for the custody of
--      property, shall be punished with imprisonment for a term which may
--      extend to 7 years, and shall also be liable to fine.
--
--      (The marginal heading is "Theft in dwelling house, etc."; Woon calls the
--      offence "theft in dwelling", and so does this module.)
--
-- The seventeen illustrations to s 378 are not reproduced in full. The three
-- this module's bench turns on are, verbatim:
--
--      (g) A finds a ring lying on the high road, not in the possession of any
--          person. A by taking it commits no theft, though he may commit
--          criminal misappropriation of property.
--      (i) A delivers his watch to Z, a jeweller, to be regulated. Z carries it
--          to his shop. A, not owing to the jeweller any debt for which the
--          jeweller might lawfully detain the watch as a security, enters the
--          shop openly, takes his watch by force out of Z's hand, and carries it
--          away. Here A, though he may have committed criminal trespass and
--          assault, has not committed theft, inasmuch as what he did was not
--          done dishonestly.
--      (q) A gains access to Z's bank account and dishonestly transfers money
--          from Z's account to A's own account without Z's knowledge or consent.
--          A commits theft.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shape of the section
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- s 378 is ONE sentence and a pure CONJUNCTION — seven elements, every one of
-- which must hold. There is no disjunctive limb anywhere in it. That is what
-- makes it the cleanest ladder in the family, and it is why the illustrations
-- read as they do: each of (g), (i), (m), (n) and (p) knocks out exactly one
-- leaf and says "A has not committed theft".
--
--   MENS REA     intending to take + dishonestly (s 24)
--   THE THING    any movable property (s 22; Explanations 1, 2 and 6)
--   THE HOLDING  out of the possession of any person (Illustration (g): a ring
--                on the high road is in nobody's possession, so there is
--                nothing to take it out of)
--   THE CONSENT  without that person's consent (Explanation 5: express or
--                implied, and Illustration (m) turns on the implied kind)
--   THE ACT      moves that property (Explanations 3, 4 and 6 tell you what
--                counts as moving)
--   THE PURPOSE  in order to such taking (Illustration (a): the severance is a
--                theft because it was done "in order to such taking"; a moving
--                that is not for that purpose is not theft)
--
-- Two calls this encoding makes, recorded rather than hidden:
--
--   1. `without that person's consent` is a leaf of NEGATIVE polarity, not a
--      positive `consent` leaf under a NOT. The statute's own words are the
--      answerable question — an investigating officer establishes absence of
--      consent, not presence — and a positive leaf would force either a double
--      negative in the ladder's reading ("without … not consent") or an inert
--      string that shadows its own leaf. The @desc carries Explanation 5, which
--      is where the difficulty actually lives.
--   2. `movable property` is a leaf, not a nested call. s 22 is an ontology, not
--      a rule (see penal-code-general.l4, which encodes ss 22–25 and says so);
--      there is nothing to call.
--
-- The particulars the charge recites but the section does not test (who the
-- victim is, what was taken) are STRING fields on the same record, so one
-- record is both the ladder's input and the charge's.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The shape of s 380: two disjunctions in series, not a flat conjunction
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Of the sections this module encodes, s 380 is the first with any disjunctive
-- limb, and it has two of them:
--
--      theft  AND  ( building OR tent OR vessel )
--             AND  ( used as a human dwelling OR used for the custody of property )
--
-- Woon, Essential Criminal Law ch. 8 p. 185, draws exactly that: `Theft`, then
-- `in a` over three parallel boxes {building, tent, vessel}, then `used` over
-- two parallel boxes {as a human dwelling, for custody of property}, then the
-- consequent. Two OR-groups, in series with each other and with the theft. His
-- `in a` and `used` boxes are inert connectives, and they are inert strings
-- here.
--
-- Writing this as a flat conjunction of five leaves would be a WRONG ANSWER
-- rather than a difference of style: it would demand a building that was at
-- once a human dwelling AND a store, and would refuse the commonest case there
-- is. Woon, same page: shop theft "is captured by the offence of theft in
-- dwelling because shops are generally buildings used for the custody of
-- property". `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket` below is the fixture to read
-- first, and `Tan Mei Ling, in a building used for neither` is the control that
-- proves the two groups are in series rather than fused.
--
-- The statute's "which building, tent or vessel" is an express co-reference:
-- the thing used as a dwelling or a store is the same thing the theft happened
-- in. One record carries one locus, and both groups are asked of it.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Why s 380 nests a theft, and why there is no `commits theft in dwelling`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- `Theft in Dwelling Facts` NESTS a `Theft Facts` rather than adding fields to
-- it, for the reason `Robbery Facts` nests one: the aggravating section adds to
-- a theft, it does not restate one. Nesting is also the cheap direction here.
-- A `Theft Facts WITH` literal must list every field, and there are three in
-- this module and three more in robbery-390-392.l4; extending the record would
-- have meant editing all six, and nesting edits none.
--
-- Woon and this row characterise s 380 differently, and the difference is worth
-- recording rather than papering over. p. 185 says in terms that "theft in
-- dwelling is defined in section 380 PC", and his figure's consequent node
-- reads `Commits Theft in dwelling` — so he treats s 380 as defining an offence
-- whose first element is theft. This row treats it as a PUNISHING section that
-- carries extra elements of its own, like ss 379A, 381 and 382. s 378 is the
-- only section in the group that says "is said to commit"; s 380 says only
-- "shall be punished", and a charge is laid under the punishing section (CPC
-- s 123(4)). So Woon's `Commits Theft in dwelling` maps to `offence under
-- s 380` here, and NO `commits theft in dwelling` defining ladder is created.
-- The two readings agree on everything that must be proved; they disagree only
-- about which section is said to define it.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 125 and the recital: theft is the section that states NO manner
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- CPC 2010 s 125 illustration (a) is about this very offence: "A is accused of
-- the theft of a certain article at a certain time and place. The charge need
-- not set out the manner in which the theft was effected." So the recital this
-- module builds names the thing and the person it was taken from, and stops:
--
--   did commit theft of <the property>, in the possession of <one victim>
--
-- CONSTRUCTED, NOT REPORTED. Unlike the s 384 and s 392 recitals in this row,
-- this wording is not taken from a judgment — the bench has no reported s 379
-- charge in it. The form follows Singapore charging practice ("in the
-- possession of one …") and is chosen to track s 378's own "out of the
-- possession of any person", so that the one element the recital names beyond
-- the property is an element the ladder actually tests. Treat the asserted body
-- below as a fixture of this encoding, not as an oracle.
--
-- The s 380 recital is the same body with the locus added, and is CONSTRUCTED
-- on the same footing:
--
--   did commit theft of <the property>, in the possession of <one victim>,
--   in a building used for the custody of property

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `penal-code-general`

§ `Penal Code 1871 — theft`

§§ `The facts of one taking`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 378 — the operative atoms, and the particulars CPC s 124 requires
DECLARE `Theft Facts` HAS
    particulars           IS A Particulars @desc Who is charged, and when and where the offence was committed
    victim                IS A STRING  @desc The name of the person out of whose possession the property was taken, as it is to appear in the charge — for example "Ng Juin Chye Joel"
    `victim described as` IS A STRING  @desc How the charge describes that person, after the name — for example "the owner of the said stall"; leave blank if not needed
    `victim pronoun`      IS A Pronoun @desc The pronoun the charge uses for that person
    `the property`        IS A STRING  @desc What was taken, as it is to read after "did commit theft of" — for example "a handphone valued at about $450/-"

    -- "intending to take dishonestly"
    `intending to take`   IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused intend to TAKE the property — to assume it as their own — as opposed to merely handling, damaging or using it where it lay?
    dishonestly           IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused act dishonestly — intending a wrongful gain or a wrongful loss, or in a way reasonable and honest people would call dishonest, knowing that (s 24)? Illustration (i): taking back one's own watch from a jeweller who has no lien over it is not dishonest.

    -- "any movable property"
    `movable property`    IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the thing taken movable property — property of any description other than land, things attached to the earth, and things permanently fastened to anything so attached (s 22)? A thing attached to the earth becomes capable of theft as soon as it is severed (Explanation 1); money in a bank account is (Explanation 6).

    -- "out of the possession of any person without that person's consent"
    `out of the possession of any person`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the property in the possession of some person at the moment it was moved? Illustration (g): a ring lying on the high road is in nobody's possession, so there is nothing to take it out of — that is misappropriation, not theft.
    `without that person's consent`
                          IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the property moved WITHOUT the consent of the person in possession? Consent may be express or implied, and may be given by the person in possession or by anyone having authority for that purpose, express or implied (Explanation 5). Illustration (m): a friend who borrows a book from an absent friend's library may well have implied consent.

    -- "moves that property in order to such taking"
    `moves that property`     IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused move the property? Removing an obstacle that prevented it from moving, or separating it from another thing, counts as much as carrying it (Explanation 3); causing an animal to move it counts (Explanation 4); transferring intangible property from one account to another counts (Explanation 6).
    `in order to such taking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the moving done FOR THE PURPOSE of that taking? Illustration (a): severing a tree is theft the moment it is severed, because the severing was done in order to the taking — a moving done for some other reason is not theft.

-- The particulars are NOT repeated here. There is one offence and one occasion,
-- so a second copy on the outer record could only ever diverge from the one on
-- the nested theft; `charge under s 380` reads `f's theft's particulars`.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 380 — the theft, and the place it was committed in
DECLARE `Theft in Dwelling Facts` HAS
    theft IS A `Theft Facts` @desc The taking itself. s 380 adds a place to a theft, so the seven elements of s 378 are asked here and asked once.

    -- "in any building, tent or vessel"
    building IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the theft committed in a BUILDING? By far the commonest limb, and it catches theft from shops and other commercial premises as much as theft from homes.
    tent     IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it committed in a TENT? The rarest limb.
    vessel   IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it committed in a VESSEL? Not a rare limb in Singapore, which is a major port.

    -- "which building, tent or vessel is used as a human dwelling, or for the
    --  custody of property"
    `as a human dwelling` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that SAME building, tent or vessel used as a human dwelling? "Human dwelling" is not defined in the Penal Code 1871, so the ordinary meaning governs: somewhere a human being lives.
    `for the custody of property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it used FOR THE CUSTODY OF PROPERTY? A shop is — goods are kept there — and so is a warehouse or a store room. This is the limb that makes shop theft an offence under s 380.

§§ `s 378 — theft`

-- Seven leaves, all conjoined: s 378 has no disjunctive limb. The inert strings
-- are the sentence's own connective glue, and reading the rule top to bottom
-- reconstructs the section with no word appearing twice.
@export Whether the accused "commits theft" within section 378 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Theft Facts` @desc The taking — what was taken, from whom, with what intent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commits theft` f IF
        "Whoever,"
   ...  f's `intending to take`
   ...  f's dishonestly
   ...  "any"
   ...  f's `movable property`
   ...  f's `out of the possession of any person`
   ...  f's `without that person's consent`
   ...  f's `moves that property`
   ...  f's `in order to such taking`
   ...  ", is said to commit theft"

§§ `s 379 — punishment for theft`

@export Whether the accused has committed an offence punishable under section 379 of the Penal Code 1871 (theft)
GIVEN f IS A `Theft Facts` @desc The taking — what was taken, from whom, with what intent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offence under s 379` f IF
        "Whoever"
   ...  `commits theft` f

§§ `s 380 — punishment for theft in a dwelling house, etc.`

-- Two OR-groups, in series with each other and with the theft. Reading the rule
-- top to bottom reconstructs s 380 with no word appearing twice, and the shape
-- is the one Woon draws on p. 185 — see "The shape of s 380" in the header.
@export Whether the accused has committed an offence punishable under section 380 of the Penal Code 1871 (theft in a building, tent or vessel used as a human dwelling or for the custody of property)
GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts` @desc The taking, and the place it was committed in
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offence under s 380` f IF
        "Whoever"
   ...  `commits theft` (f's theft)
   ...  "in any"
   ...  (    f's building
        ..   f's tent
        OR   f's vessel )
   ...  ", which building, tent or vessel is used"
   ...  (    f's `as a human dwelling`
        OR   f's `for the custody of property` )

§§ `The charges`

-- No manner clause: CPC s 125 illustration (a) says a theft charge need not set
-- out how the theft was effected. The recital names the thing and the person it
-- was taken from, and stops there.
GIVEN f IS A `Theft Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the recital of the theft` f MEANS
    CONCAT "did commit theft of ", f's `the property`,
           ", in the possession of ", (`one` (f's victim) (f's `victim described as`))

-- The elements, named for the refusal. s 378 is a pure conjunction, so there is
-- deliberately no "the elements taken together" row of the kind the cheating
-- module needs: if every leaf holds, the ladder holds, and such a row could
-- never fire on its own.
GIVEN f IS A `Theft Facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`elements of theft not made out` f MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (`missing unless` (f's `intending to take`) "intending to take"),
        (`missing unless` (f's dishonestly) "dishonestly (section 24)"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `movable property`) "any movable property (section 22)"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `out of the possession of any person`) "out of the possession of any person"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `without that person's consent`) "without that person's consent"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `moves that property`) "moves that property"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `in order to such taking`) "in order to such taking"))

@export Frame a charge under section 379 of the Penal Code 1871 (theft), or say why none can be framed
GIVEN f IS A `Theft Facts` @desc The taking, and the particulars of the charge
GIVETH A Charge
`charge under s 379` f MEANS
    `frame the charge` (f's particulars) "379" "theft"
        "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 3 years, or with fine, or with both"
        (`offence under s 379` f)
        (`the recital of the theft` f)
        (`elements of theft not made out` f)

-- The locus, for the s 380 recital. Each group is a disjunction, so more than
-- one limb can hold at once — a houseboat is a vessel used as a human dwelling,
-- and a shophouse may be both a dwelling and a store — and the recital names
-- every limb the officer established rather than choosing one for them.
GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the place the theft was committed in` f MEANS
    `joined with` " or " (concat (LIST
        (`included if` (f's building) "building"),
        (`included if` (f's tent) "tent"),
        (`included if` (f's vessel) "vessel")))

GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`what that place was used for` f MEANS
    `joined with` " or " (concat (LIST
        (`included if` (f's `as a human dwelling`) "as a human dwelling"),
        (`included if` (f's `for the custody of property`) "for the custody of property")))

GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts`
GIVETH A STRING
`the recital of the theft in dwelling` f MEANS
    CONCAT (`the recital of the theft` (f's theft)),
           ", in a ", (`the place the theft was committed in` f),
           " used ", (`what that place was used for` f)

-- s 380's elements are the theft's, plus two disjunctions. The theft is named
-- as ONE row rather than leaf by leaf, for the reason `elements of robbery not
-- made out` gives in robbery-390-392.l4: a reader who needs the individual leaf
-- runs `charge under s 379` on the nested record, which names them one by one.
-- Each disjunction is named by the statute's own words for the whole group,
-- because naming one disjunct would name a limb the facts never relied on.
GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`elements of theft in dwelling not made out` f MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (`missing unless` (`commits theft` (f's theft)) "theft (section 378)"),
        (`missing unless` (f's building OR f's tent OR f's vessel)
            "in any building, tent or vessel"),
        (`missing unless` (f's `as a human dwelling` OR f's `for the custody of property`)
            "which building, tent or vessel is used as a human dwelling, or for the custody of property"))

@export Frame a charge under section 380 of the Penal Code 1871 (theft in a dwelling house, etc.), or say why none can be framed
GIVEN f IS A `Theft in Dwelling Facts` @desc The taking, the place it was committed in, and the particulars of the charge
GIVETH A Charge
`charge under s 380` f MEANS
    `frame the charge` (f's theft's particulars) "380" "theft in dwelling"
        "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, and shall also be liable to fine"
        (`offence under s 380` f)
        (`the recital of the theft in dwelling` f)
        (`elements of theft in dwelling not made out` f)

§§ `Illustration (q) — the bank transfer`

-- CONSTRUCTED FIXTURE, not a reported charge. Illustration (q) to s 378, laid
-- against invented particulars so the recital has something to recite:
--
--      (q) A gains access to Z's bank account and dishonestly transfers money
--          from Z's account to A's own account without Z's knowledge or consent.
--          A commits theft.
--
-- Explanation 6 is what makes it theft rather than nothing: money in an account
-- is intangible property, and transferring it from one account to another IS a
-- moving. Every one of the seven leaves is TRUE, so the charge frames.
`Lim Wei Ming` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "Lim Wei Ming"
                               date               IS "the 3rd day of March 2021"
                               time               IS "11.15 pm"
                               place              IS "Blk 123 Ang Mo Kio Avenue 3"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    victim                IS "Goh Siew Lan"
    `victim described as` IS ""
    `victim pronoun`      IS she
    `the property`        IS "moneys amounting to $4,500/-"
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS TRUE
    `without that person's consent`       IS TRUE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

-- Illustration (i), on the same particulars: the accused took back what was
-- already his from someone with no lien over it. Every other leaf stands; the
-- taking was not dishonest, and s 378 fails on that one leaf.
`Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s particulars
    victim                IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s victim
    `victim described as` IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `victim described as`
    `victim pronoun`      IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `victim pronoun`
    `the property`        IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `the property`
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS FALSE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS TRUE
    `without that person's consent`       IS TRUE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

-- Illustration (g), on the same particulars: the thing was in nobody's
-- possession when it was moved. Two leaves fall, not one — there being no
-- person in possession, there is also no consent of such a person to be
-- without, and the refusal names both.
`Lim Wei Ming, not in anyone's possession` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s particulars
    victim                IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s victim
    `victim described as` IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `victim described as`
    `victim pronoun`      IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `victim pronoun`
    `the property`        IS `Lim Wei Ming`'s `the property`
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS FALSE
    `without that person's consent`       IS FALSE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

§§ `Section 380 — shop theft, and theft from a home`

-- CONSTRUCTED FIXTURES, not reported charges: the bench this row was built on
-- has no s 380 case in it. Woon, Essential Criminal Law ch. 8 p. 185, is the
-- authority for why shop theft leads: it is "the more common variant of theft
-- in dwelling", caught "because shops are generally buildings used for the
-- custody of property". He also records that it is not clear from the SPF
-- Annual Crime Brief 2022 whether shop theft is prosecuted under s 379 or
-- s 380, and that cases may straddle both — usually charged under s 380 and
-- reduced to s 379 on a plea of guilt. That is why `applicable charges` in
-- charge-sheet.l4 lists BOTH on these facts, s 380 first.
`Tan Mei Ling` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "Tan Mei Ling"
                               date               IS "the 14th day of June 2023"
                               time               IS "4.30 pm"
                               place              IS "Blk 505 Jurong West Street 52"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    victim                IS "Chua Hock Seng"
    `victim described as` IS "the manager of the said supermarket"
    `victim pronoun`      IS he
    `the property`        IS "one bottle of perfume valued at about $89/-"
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS TRUE
    `without that person's consent`       IS TRUE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

-- The motivating case. A supermarket is a building used for the custody of
-- property, so both groups hold, and so does the theft: s 380 is made out, and
-- s 379 is made out on the same taking.
`Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Tan Mei Ling`
    building                      IS TRUE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS FALSE
    `for the custody of property` IS TRUE

-- CONSTRUCTED CONTROL on the FIRST disjunction: the same taking, supposed to
-- have happened at an open-air stall in the car park. The theft is untouched
-- and s 379 still lies; there is no building, tent or vessel, so s 380 does
-- not. This is the assertion that fails if the locus is ever dropped from the
-- rule — which is the whole difference between s 379 and s 380.
`Tan Mei Ling, in the open` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Tan Mei Ling`
    building                      IS FALSE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS FALSE
    `for the custody of property` IS FALSE

-- CONSTRUCTED CONTROL on the SECOND disjunction: a building, but one used
-- neither as a human dwelling nor for the custody of property — a disused
-- pavilion standing empty. The first group holds and the second does not, so
-- s 380 fails. This fixture is what proves the two groups are in SERIES: a flat
-- five-leaf conjunction would fail here too, but it would also fail on
-- `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`, and the pair together pin the shape.
`Tan Mei Ling, in a building used for neither` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Tan Mei Ling`
    building                      IS TRUE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS FALSE
    `for the custody of property` IS FALSE

-- CONSTRUCTED CONTROL on the NESTED THEFT. She walked out of the same
-- supermarket with an item she believed she had already paid for. Both of
-- s 380's own groups hold; the theft does not, so the offence does not, and the
-- refusal names the theft rather than the place. Woon, p. 185: "theft is an
-- element of theft in dwelling, and therefore, the elements of a charge of
-- theft simpliciter also need to be proved to establish theft in dwelling."
`Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS `Tan Mei Ling`'s particulars
    victim                IS `Tan Mei Ling`'s victim
    `victim described as` IS `Tan Mei Ling`'s `victim described as`
    `victim pronoun`      IS `Tan Mei Ling`'s `victim pronoun`
    `the property`        IS `Tan Mei Ling`'s `the property`
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS FALSE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS TRUE
    `without that person's consent`       IS TRUE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

`Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own, in the supermarket` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own`
    building                      IS TRUE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS FALSE
    `for the custody of property` IS TRUE

-- The variant the section is named for: a taking from someone's home.
`Ravi s/o Muniandy` MEANS `Theft Facts` WITH
    particulars           IS Particulars WITH
                               accused            IS "Ravi s/o Muniandy"
                               date               IS "the 2nd day of September 2023"
                               time               IS "10.45 am"
                               place              IS "Blk 118 Bishan Street 12 #05-233"
                               `co-accused`       IS ""
                               `common intention` IS FALSE
    victim                IS "Ong Bee Choo"
    `victim described as` IS ""
    `victim pronoun`      IS she
    `the property`        IS "a gold necklace valued at about $1,200/-"
    `intending to take`   IS TRUE
    dishonestly           IS TRUE
    `movable property`    IS TRUE
    `out of the possession of any person` IS TRUE
    `without that person's consent`       IS TRUE
    `moves that property`     IS TRUE
    `in order to such taking` IS TRUE

`Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the flat` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Ravi s/o Muniandy`
    building                      IS TRUE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS TRUE
    `for the custody of property` IS FALSE

-- CONSTRUCTED CONTROLS on the two disjuncts no real fact pattern in this row
-- reaches. Without them `tent` and `vessel` would be leaves that no fixture
-- ever turns on, and an encoding that dropped either would still run green.
-- Woon, p. 185: theft in dwelling "could also occur on ships, since Singapore
-- is a major port. It goes without saying that the tent variant of the offence
-- is much rarer." The same taking is supposed to have happened aboard a
-- houseboat, and in a tent at a campsite; both are lived in.
`Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the vessel` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Ravi s/o Muniandy`
    building                      IS FALSE
    tent                          IS FALSE
    vessel                        IS TRUE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS TRUE
    `for the custody of property` IS FALSE

`Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the tent` MEANS `Theft in Dwelling Facts` WITH
    theft                         IS `Ravi s/o Muniandy`
    building                      IS FALSE
    tent                          IS TRUE
    vessel                        IS FALSE
    `as a human dwelling`         IS TRUE
    `for the custody of property` IS FALSE

§§ `Directives`

#EVAL `commits theft` `Lim Wei Ming`
#EVAL (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly`)'s refusal
#EVAL (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not in anyone's possession`)'s refusal

-- Illustration (q): every leaf holds, so the charge frames.
#ASSERT `commits theft` `Lim Wei Ming`
#ASSERT `offence under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT `the recital of the theft` `Lim Wei Ming` EQUALS "did commit theft of moneys amounting to $4,500/-, in the possession of one Goh Siew Lan"
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming`)'s text EQUALS "You, Lim Wei Ming, are charged that you, on or about the 3rd day of March 2021, at about 11.15 pm, at Blk 123 Ang Mo Kio Avenue 3, Singapore, did commit theft of moneys amounting to $4,500/-, in the possession of one Goh Siew Lan, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 379 of the Penal Code 1871."
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming`)'s punishment EQUALS "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 3 years, or with fine, or with both"

-- Illustration (i): not dishonestly. One leaf off, no charge, and the refusal
-- names the leaf.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits theft` `Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly`)
#ASSERT NOT ((`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly`)'s `made out`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly`)'s text EQUALS ""
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not dishonestly`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 379 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of theft (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: dishonestly (section 24)."

-- Illustration (g): in nobody's possession. Two leaves off, and the refusal
-- names both, in the order the section states them.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits theft` `Lim Wei Ming, not in anyone's possession`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 379` `Lim Wei Ming, not in anyone's possession`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 379 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of theft (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: out of the possession of any person; without that person's consent."

#EVAL `offence under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`
#EVAL (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`)'s text
#EVAL (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the open`)'s refusal
#EVAL (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own, in the supermarket`)'s refusal

-- Shop theft: a supermarket is a building used for the custody of property.
-- s 380 is made out, and so is s 379 on the very same taking — the straddle
-- Woon describes at p. 185.
#ASSERT `offence under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`
#ASSERT `offence under s 379` `Tan Mei Ling`
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`)'s `made out`
#ASSERT `the recital of the theft in dwelling` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket` EQUALS "did commit theft of one bottle of perfume valued at about $89/-, in the possession of one Chua Hock Seng, the manager of the said supermarket, in a building used for the custody of property"
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`)'s text EQUALS "You, Tan Mei Ling, are charged that you, on or about the 14th day of June 2023, at about 4.30 pm, at Blk 505 Jurong West Street 52, Singapore, did commit theft of one bottle of perfume valued at about $89/-, in the possession of one Chua Hock Seng, the manager of the said supermarket, in a building used for the custody of property, and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section 380 of the Penal Code 1871."
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the supermarket`)'s punishment EQUALS "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, and shall also be liable to fine"

-- Theft from a home, and the two rarer limbs. Each of these picks a different
-- disjunct out of the first group, and the recital reports the one that held.
#ASSERT `offence under s 380` `Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the flat`
#ASSERT `the recital of the theft in dwelling` `Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the flat` EQUALS "did commit theft of a gold necklace valued at about $1,200/-, in the possession of one Ong Bee Choo, in a building used as a human dwelling"
#ASSERT `offence under s 380` `Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the vessel`
#ASSERT `the recital of the theft in dwelling` `Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the vessel` EQUALS "did commit theft of a gold necklace valued at about $1,200/-, in the possession of one Ong Bee Choo, in a vessel used as a human dwelling"
#ASSERT `offence under s 380` `Ravi s/o Muniandy, in the tent`

-- In the open: the taking is a theft and s 379 lies, but neither group of s 380
-- holds, and the refusal names both of them.
#ASSERT NOT (`offence under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the open`)
#ASSERT `offence under s 379` (`Tan Mei Ling, in the open`'s theft)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in the open`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 380 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of theft in dwelling (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: in any building, tent or vessel; which building, tent or vessel is used as a human dwelling, or for the custody of property."

-- A building used for neither purpose: the first group holds, the second does
-- not, and only the second is named. The groups are in series.
#ASSERT NOT (`offence under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in a building used for neither`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, in a building used for neither`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 380 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of theft in dwelling (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: which building, tent or vessel is used as a human dwelling, or for the custody of property."

-- Both groups hold and the theft does not. The refusal names the theft, and a
-- reader who wants the leaf runs `charge under s 379` on the nested record.
#ASSERT NOT (`commits theft` `Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own`)
#ASSERT NOT (`offence under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own, in the supermarket`)
#ASSERT (`charge under s 380` `Tan Mei Ling, mistaken for her own, in the supermarket`)'s refusal EQUALS "A charge under section 380 would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of theft in dwelling (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: theft (section 378)."
